// rbac domain -- capability-catalog row predicates (traits).
//
// Under the current grammar a payload-only predicate is a `trait`
// (a concept-agnostic row predicate usable in a query `filter`); a
// `spec` is reserved for actor.* authorization predicates usable in a
// `requires` slot. These traits over the v1:rbac:capability concept
// compile to SQL WHERE fragments and push down to the database. They
// are the composable filter vocabulary the capability queries
// (queries.memql) can stitch together, and the building blocks the
// E1.6 enforcement resolver (memql#2074) leans on to answer "does role
// R hold an ALLOW for (verb, resourceType) with no overriding DENY?".
//
// The CALLER-side rank/self governance predicates land as logic in
// E1.3 (memql#2071); they are a different evaluation flavor (in-process
// against the (actor, target) pair) and live in dsl/rbac/logic.memql
// there.

/// Row predicate: the capability is an ALLOW grant. allow capabilities confer the (verb,
/// resourceType); deny capabilities revoke it. The enforcement resolver (E1.6) treats a role as
/// holding a grant when an allow row matches AND no deny row does.
trait capabilityIsAllow {
  return effect == "allow"
}

/// Row predicate: the capability is a DENY grant -- an explicit revocation that wins over any allow
/// for the same (role, verb, resourceType). Lets a role carve a hole in an otherwise-broad grant
/// set.
trait capabilityIsDeny {
  return effect == "deny"
}

/// Row predicate: the capability is a base-role (predefined, seed-authored) grant. The immutable
/// grant set E1.2 (memql#2070) marks read-only at runtime. User/custom-role grants are
/// predefined=false.
trait capabilityIsPredefined {
  return predefined == true
}
